{"assets":["/editorial-assets/01691dfbe0db367570e7df434ad1ba03d08d4c19e6b1dfacdc3283e1e887822d.png","/editorial-assets/c45549ba418884b29e5fc166eee11a246d92415c3fa46ca1e9d5f1aa4e5143a0.png","/editorial-assets/ddd5738be5a3d1231284bfb95cb182fa85a57b17da838a541949b68825b060c6.png","/editorial-assets/91188b18a662d9b099cf4a4e56444204130bf0ca294cef07c788a6e9f438ff83.png","/editorial-assets/626c5dad5ff32c196b8f8b86800861ee78f320f14971beeffb14879c80b36600.png","/editorial-assets/c7e99f2ccd07da359fe99567d0a19e6a43998a54d7869d130258d5a3ecb1b3a1.png","/editorial-assets/932eebd757c29379ddec3407c179eb72230ed8bb803ab0c6f960f5016ceec8d1.png"],"contestId":"9","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A. "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/9/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Die Roll "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"If the maximum of Yakko's and Wakko's points is a, then Dot will win, if she has not less than a points. So the probability of her win is (6 - (a-1)) / 6. Since there are only 6 values for a, you can simply hardcode the answers."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B. "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/9/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Running Student "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"It is simple to calculate the time "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" that the Student will need, if he gets off the bus at the i-th stop: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the time he will drive on the bus, and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/01691dfbe0db367570e7df434ad1ba03d08d4c19e6b1dfacdc3283e1e887822d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the time he will need to get from the i-th stop to the University. We need to choose indices with minimal possible "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", and among them - the index with minimal possible "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", that is, with maximal "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", that is (since the coordinates of bus stops are already ordered) with maximal i."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Note that due to precision issues, you should be careful when you compare "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":": the condition "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" should be written in the form |"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"| < ε for some small ε."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C. "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/9/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Hexadecimal's Numbers "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"Brute force solution, when you try each number from 1 to n, will not fit into the time limit."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Note, however, that all good numbers have at most 10 digits, and each of the digits is 0 or 1. That is, there are at most 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"10"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" binary strings to check. Each of these strings is a number from 1 to 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"10"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 in binary representation. So the algorithm is the following: for each number from 1 to 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"10"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 write its binary  representation, read it as if it was decimal representation and compare the result to n."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D. "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/9/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"How many trees? "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"Denote by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nh"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" the number of binary search trees on n nodes with height equal to h. We will derive a recurrent formula for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nh"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". For the base case note that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"00"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1 (empty tree), and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0 if i>0."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Now take any binary search tree on n nodes with height equal to h. Let m be the number written at its root, 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". The left subtree is a binary search tree on m-1 nodes, and the right subtree is a binary search tree on n-m nodes. The maximal of their heights must be equal to h-1. Consider 2 subcases:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"1. The height of the left subtree is equal to h-1. There are "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" such trees. The right subtree can have any height from 0 to h-1, so there are "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c45549ba418884b29e5fc166eee11a246d92415c3fa46ca1e9d5f1aa4e5143a0.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" such trees. Since we can choose left and right subtrees independently, we have "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ddd5738be5a3d1231284bfb95cb182fa85a57b17da838a541949b68825b060c6.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" variants in this case."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"2. The height of the left subtree is less than h-1. There are "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/91188b18a662d9b099cf4a4e56444204130bf0ca294cef07c788a6e9f438ff83.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" such trees, and the right subtree must have height exactly h-1, which gives us totally "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/626c5dad5ff32c196b8f8b86800861ee78f320f14971beeffb14879c80b36600.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" variants."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"So the recurrent formula is the following: "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c7e99f2ccd07da359fe99567d0a19e6a43998a54d7869d130258d5a3ecb1b3a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"All the values "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nh"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be calculated by dynamic programming. The answer, then, is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/932eebd757c29379ddec3407c179eb72230ed8bb803ab0c6f960f5016ceec8d1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"E. "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/9/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Interesting graph and Apples "}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"text","text":"The funny ring consists of n vertices and n edges. If there is another edge except for these n, then the vertices it connects belong to more than one cycle. So, an interesting graph is just a funny ring. "},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"A graph is a funny ring if and only if the following conditions hold:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"A1. The degree of each vertex equals 2."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"A2. The graph is connected."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" Now let's figure out when a graph is not yet a funny ring, but can be transformed into a funny ring by adding edges. There are obvious necessary conditions:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"B1. m < n."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"B2. There are no cycles."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"B3. The degree of each vertex is not more than 2."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" Let's add edges so that these conditions were preserved, and the sequence of edges was lexicographically minimal. So, we add an edge (i,j) such that:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"1. The degrees of i and j are less than 2. (Otherwise we would break B3)."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"2. i and j belong to different connected components. (Otherwise we would break B2)."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"3. The pair (i,j) is lexicographically minimal."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" Let's see what we have when we can't add edges anymore. Since there are no cycles, each connected component is a tree, and therefore has at least one vertex with degree less than 2. If there are two connected components, then they could be connected by an edge without breaking B1-B3. So the graph is connected, has no cycles, and the degree of each vertex is not more than 2. This means that the obtained graph is just a walk and we can connect its end points to obtain a funny ring."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" To summarize, the algorithm is the following:"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" 1. Check if A1-A2 hold. If yes, output \"YES\" and 0."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" 2. Check if B1-B3 hold. If no, output \"NO\"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" 3. Output \"YES\" and n-m. "},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" 4. Add edges as described. When the edge (i,j) is added, output \"i j\"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":" 5. Find the only vertices i and j with degree less than 2 (they can be equal if n=1). Output \"i j\"."}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/283"}